  "narrative": "The independent validation of H10 is decisive. The signed validation estimate is +0.7925 with an independent interval of +0.763 to +0.822. Because H10 was registered with direction -1, a positive signed estimate corresponds to a negative exposed-minus-comparator difference in raw dependency units, i.e. SMARCA4-loss lines have substantially more negative SMARCA2 scores than SMARCA4-intact lines. Since more negative scores mean stronger CRISPR dependency, this confirms that SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency in this dataset. The effect is far larger than the practical 10%-scale benchmark and is consistent with the discovery estimate (+0.784) and with restricted co-occurrence analyses (e.g., +0.784 in TP53/KEAP1/MSS lines and +0.773 among TP53-loss lines). I therefore accept H10 as a scientifically meaningful dataset-level association and close it as a canonical validated result. This does not establish causality or external generalizability, but it strengthens the dataset-level association. H10 is now independently validated alongside H13.\n\nThe H3 response deadline is also clear. Discovery gave a small signed estimate favoring EGFR amplification -> stronger EGFR dependency, but the validation estimate was opposite-signed and small (-0.022, interval -0.061 to +0.017). Across both discovery and validation the magnitude is well below the practical clinical-significance threshold and the evidence is inconsistent. Therefore EGFR amplification is not supported as a meaningful predictor of EGFR dependency in this dataset, and H3 remains rejected.\n\nThe next most informative work is not additional validation of the already validated canonical associations, but robustness across technical strata and screen quality, plus one leakage check for pathway specificity. I am keeping active the Avana-library effects for USP7/TP53, SMARCA2/SMARCA4, WRN/MSI, and NFE2L2/KEAP1; the non-Avana main effects for NFE2L2, USP7, and SMARCA2; a new non-Avana NFE2L2 effect; high screen-quality main effects for NFE2L2, USP7, SMARCA2, and WRN; and H63, which tests whether TP53 loss also shifts SMARCA2 dependency. I deferred lower-priority marker interactions, adenocarcinoma-only main effects because the corresponding lineage interactions were already small, sex/culture/serum interactions, other cross-specificity tests, and broad pathway discovery tests. This focused panel asks whether the large effects survive the dominant library split and high-quality screens, and whether they leak across pathways. After these results, the likely next validation target should be H8, MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency, because its exposed cell size is smaller and independent validation would be most informative for a rare-subgroup synthetic-lethality-like association; H7 remains a secondary validation candidate.",
  "research_notes": "Accepted broad dataset-level associations: H13 KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency (independently validated); H10 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency (now independently validated); H7 TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency (accepted but not independently validated); H8 MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency (accepted but not independently validated). Restricted robustness claims H29-H32 and H56-H58 remain accepted, with conditional effects around +0.77 to +0.81. Closed/validated or completed canonical questions: H13 and H10 closed; H3 rejected and closed after inconsistent small effects. Negative/artifact controls closed: H1, H2, H4, H5, H6, H9, H11-H12, H18, H20, H33-H36, H41-H42, H47, H25-H28, H52-H55. Interaction tests for lineage subtype and most batch effects were not clinically meaningful.\n\nValidation budget: 7 new independent validation requests remain. Used/cached validations: H3, H9/H13, H10. Next validation candidate after current robustness panel: H8 (MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency) because rare exposure makes validation most valuable; H7 (TP53 -> weaker USP7) is secondary. If current robustness panel reveals library- or screen-quality heterogeneity, prioritize interaction/stratification analysis before further validation.\n\nCurrent active analysis panel: H44 USP7/TP53 in Avana; H45 SMARCA2/SMARCA4 in Avana; H46 WRN/MSI in Avana; H49 NFE2L2/KEAP1 in Avana; H50 USP7/TP53 non-Avana; H51 SMARCA2/SMARCA4 non-Avana; H59 NFE2L2/KEAP1 high screen ROC; new NFE2L2/KEAP1 non-Avana; new USP7/TP53 high screen ROC; new SMARCA2/SMARCA4 high screen ROC; new WRN/MSI high screen ROC; H63 TP53 loss with SMARCA2 dependency as leakage/specificity control. Deferred: adenocarcinoma main effects H14-H16/H43 because lineage interactions were small; marker interactions H17/H19/H37-H40; remaining library strata; sex/culture/serum interactions H60-H62; other cross-specificity H64-H65; broad pathway H66-H71. WRN non-Avana was intentionally not proposed because MSI-high within non-Avana may have under 20 exposed lines. Interpret all current accepted results as dataset-level associations, not causal effects or broadly generalizable claims."
