  "narrative": "No new discovery or validation evidence is due at this explore stage. The current evidence base strongly supports four dataset-level associations: KEAP1 loss associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency (validated), SMARCA4 loss associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency (validated), TP53 loss associated with weaker USP7 dependency, and MSI-high associated with stronger WRN dependency. The main remaining scientific questions are not whether these associations have large effects, but whether they are modified by major genetic co-states, screen-quality, or CRISPR library context, and whether they leak into biologically related dependencies. I therefore registered direct interaction tests rather than adding more subset main effects. First, I registered genetic-context interactions for the accepted effects: KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 modification by TP53-loss, TP53-loss/USP7 modification by KEAP1-loss, SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 modification by TP53-loss, and MSI-high/WRN modification by TP53-loss. These are more direct than comparing separate conditional main effects and test whether the associations behave homogeneously across TP53 or KEAP1 co-mutation status. Second, I registered screen-quality interactions using screen_roc_auc >= 0.93 versus the complement for all four accepted associations. This tests whether high-quality screens show the same effect magnitude as lower-quality screens, which is relevant to technical reliability. Third, I registered Avana-library interactions for KEAP1/NFE2L2, TP53/USP7, and SMARCA4/SMARCA2. I did not register a WRN library interaction because MSI-high within non-Avana lines is expected to be too small for stable Welch cells; for WRN, the broad and screen-quality interactions are more informative than a fragile library-stratified test. I deferred the previously registered library and screen-quality main-effect strata because the new interaction tests are more direct and statistically efficient for assessing modification. I also deferred TP53-loss with RPL11 dependency to keep the immediate focus on robustness of the four accepted large associations and one leakage control (H63, TP53-loss with SMARCA2 dependency). The accepted but validated or support conditional effects H29-H32 and H56-H58 are marked closed because they have served their purpose as supporting dataset-level associations; new refinements remain active. The anticipated direction for all new interactions is uncertain rather than assuming homogeneity, because the available conditional effects are large but not formally compared across complements. After these interaction results, if no clinically meaningful heterogeneity is found, the next independent validation candidate should be H8 MSI-high associated with stronger WRN dependency, because it is accepted, biologically interesting, and still lacks independent validation. If meaningful heterogeneity is found, the next priority should shift to refining the affected subgroup rather than validating a broad average effect. All conclusions remain dataset-level associations, not causal or broadly generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Accepted dataset-level association hierarchy: (1) Validated broad effects: H13 KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency; H10 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency. (2) Accepted but not independently validated broad effects: H7 TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency; H8 MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency. Supporting accepted conditional effects H29-H32 and H56-H58 closed because they corroborate robustness across TP53/KEAP1/MSS/MSI contexts and have effect estimates around +0.77 to +0.81. Closed negative controls and small/meaningless interactions remain H1, H2, H3, H4, H5, H6, H9, H11, H12, H18, H20, H25-H28, H33-H36, H41, H42, H47, H52-H55.\n\nCurrent active analysis plan prioritized to 12 direct heterogeneity/specificity tests: four genetic interactions (KEAP1/NFE2L2 by TP53-loss; TP53/USP7 by KEAP1-loss; SMARCA4/SMARCA2 by TP53-loss; MSI/WRN by TP53-loss); four screen-quality interactions (same four effects in screen_roc_auc >= 0.93 versus complement); three Avana-library interactions (NFE2L2, USP7, SMARCA2; WRN skipped because non-Avana MSI-high likely under 20); and H63 TP53-loss with SMARCA2 dependency as leakage/specificity control. Deferring H44-H51, H59, and H72-H75 was a deliberate de-duplication: the new interactions estimate effect modification directly rather than requiring separate subset main effects. Deferring H48 is temporary; RPL11/TP53 remains biologically interesting but lower priority than validating/robustness-testing the four accepted large effects.\n\nInterpretation plan: For the accepted broad effects, the main effect is huge. A clinically meaningful interaction would require a subgroup difference of substantial magnitude, ideally around or above 0.1 on the dependency-score scale and clinically meaningful relative to the outcome scale; small statistically detectable interactions should be treated as technical or non-meaningful heterogeneity. If genetic or screen-quality interactions are near zero, that strengthens broad association claims. If interactions are large, the next step is subgroup-specific effect estimates and possibly targeted validation, not broad validation. Library interactions for NFE2L2/USP7/SMARCA2 are less critical than for WRN, but useful to rule out library-specific artifact. H63 should be judged as a specificity/leakage test: a near-zero estimate would support pathway-specificity of accepted effects; a very large estimate would suggest shared cell-state or technical confounding and would deprioritize biological interpretation.\n\nValidation budget: 7 new validation requests remain. Already used/cached: H3, H9/H13, H10. Next validation priority after current interaction panel: H8 MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency, especially if screen-quality and TP53-interaction tests are non-heterogeneous. H7 TP53 -> USP7 remains secondary. Avoid further validation of H13 or H10 unless a later analysis unexpectedly contradicts their validated evidence."
