  "narrative": "This panel mostly strengthens the specificity of the accepted association set rather than revealing new drivers. H95 tests TP53 loss on STAT3 dependency with direction -1, meaning that a positive signed estimate would support stronger STAT3 dependency in TP53-loss lines. The observed signed estimate is -0.0124 [-0.0370, 0.0121], which is tiny, crosses zero, and points away from the proposed stronger-dependency direction. I reject and close H95. This is useful specificity evidence: the previously validated TP53-USP7 association is not part of a broad TP53-dependent STAT3 effect, while PTEN-STAT3 remains independent of TP53 status.\n\nH104 is the only new meaningful positive result. It tests PTEN loss on STAT3 dependency within high-quality screens (screen_roc_auc >= 0.93) with direction -1. The signed estimate is +0.2440 [0.1834, 0.3047]. Because the claim direction is -1, this corresponds to a negative raw contrast: PTEN-loss lines have a more negative STAT3 dependency score, i.e. stronger STAT3 dependency. The magnitude is well above the 0.1 practical benchmark for dependency-score contrasts and the interval excludes zero. I accept and close H104 as a dataset-level robustness context for the validated PTEN-STAT3 association (H67/H96/H97). I do not request independent validation of H104 because the parent PTEN-STAT3 main effect has already been independently validated, and I would prefer to preserve the remaining validation budget unless a new primary association emerges.\n\nThe RPL11 adjacency tests are null. CDKN2A loss on RPL11 dependency, H116, gives +0.0023 [-0.0175, 0.0220] with direction -1, and MTAP loss on RPL11, H126, gives -0.0017 [-0.0218, 0.0183]. Both are near zero, include zero, and are far below the practical benchmark. I reject and close both. This sharpens the CDKN2A-MDM2 finding: it is not generalized to RPL11 or to the common CDKN2A-region co-deletion marker MTAP.\n\nThe new mechanistic main-effect probes are also null. RB1 loss and WRN dependency, H117, gives +0.0154 [-0.0216, 0.0525]; NF1 loss and KRAS dependency, H118, gives -0.0202 [-0.0491, 0.0086]; and ERBB2 amplification and EGFR dependency, H119, gives +0.0032 [-0.0231, 0.0296]. All intervals cross zero and absolute effects are far below 0.1. I reject and close them. The MSI-high effect remains WRN-specific rather than a broad replication-stress pattern involving RB1, DHX9, POLR2A, KRAS, or EGFR-ERBB2 family vulnerability.\n\nThe interaction tests do not support context-specific modification of the accepted associations. ARID1A co-loss does not meaningfully modify SMARCA4 loss on SMARCA2 dependency: H120 signed +0.0373 [-0.0148, 0.0894]. SMARCA4 loss does not meaningfully modify KEAP1 loss on NFE2L2 dependency: H121 signed -0.0089 [-0.0591, 0.0413]. MYC amplification does not meaningfully modify CDKN2A loss on MDM2 dependency: H122 signed +0.0107 [-0.0838, 0.1053]. STK11 loss does not support a synergistic increase of MSI-high on WRN dependency: H123 signed -0.0490 [-0.1289, 0.0308], which points away from the proposed direction. STK11 loss also does not support modification of PTEN loss on STAT3 dependency: H124 signed -0.0116 [-0.1482, 0.1250], a very wide interval centered near zero. I reject and close H120 through H124. These results argue that the main accepted associations are broadly stable across these co-alteration contexts, though this does not exclude untested interactions or causal mechanisms.\n\nOverall, the evidence supports a focused and internally consistent set of dataset-level associations: TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency, PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency, and CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2 dependency. The new nulls strengthen specificity, while H104 adds high-quality-screen robustness for PTEN-STAT3. All conclusions remain associations within this supplied dataset, not causal