  "narrative": "No evidence assessments are due in this explore step. The currently supported dataset-level associations are fairly well characterized: TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency; KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency; SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency; PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency; and CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2 dependency. Many robustness and co-alteration interaction tests have already been performed, and several of the remaining active claims are context-specific refinements of these validated effects. Because the analysis budget is limited, I am deferring several lower-priority robustness or co-alteration claims -- H21, H22, H23, H45, H46, and H59 -- to make room for new biologically motivated probes. These deferred claims remain unresolved but not refuted; they can be reactivated if needed for specificity or robustness later.\n\nThe new proposals focus on two axes that are not yet fully mapped: the MDM2/p53 stress axis and the STAT3 upstream-crosstalk axis, plus one specificity check for the USP7 association.\n\nFirst, MYC amplification can generate ribosome-biogenesis and replication/proliferation stress, often engaging ARF-MDM2-p53 responses. I therefore test MYC amplification against MDM2 dependency within TP53-wildtype lines. This context is chosen because a MYC-MDM2 relationship would be most expected when p53 is intact. The claim direction is -1: a positive signed estimate would mean MYC-amplified TP53-wildtype lines have a more negative MDM2 dependency score, i.e. stronger MDM2 dependency. If this is null, it would strengthen the specificity of CDKN2A-MDM2 rather than suggesting a broad p53-axis MDM2 association.\n\nSecond, PTEN loss activates PI3K-AKT signaling and can modulate MDM2 activity through AKT-mediated phosphorylation. I test PTEN loss against MDM2 dependency, again within TP53-wildtype lines, because MDM2 dependency is likely most interpretable when p53 pathway output is preserved. The direction is -1, with an uncertain anticipated raw direction. A positive signed estimate would indicate stronger MDM2 dependency in PTEN-loss/TP53-wildtype lines; a null result would help confine the PTEN association to STAT3 and reduce concern that PTEN loss causes broad p53-axis dependency changes.\n\nThird, the validated PTEN-STAT3 association invites testing whether STAT3 dependency is a broader node for other growth-factor/RAS/NRF2 pathway perturbations. I test NF1 loss against STAT3 dependency with direction -1 because NF1 loss can increase RAS/MAPK signaling and may produce stronger STAT3 dependence. I also test ERBB2 amplification against STAT3 dependency with direction -1 because HER2-amplified cells can activate STAT3-associated survival signaling. Both would be meaningful if they produce signed estimates well away from zero and around or above the practical 0.1 dependency-score scale, but they are also useful as specificity controls if null: the PTEN-STAT3 association may be pathway-selective rather than a general RTK/RAS signal.\n\nFourth, I test KEAP1 loss against STAT3 dependency. NRF2 and STAT3 can interact through oxidative-stress and inflammatory-survival programs, but the expected direction is uncertain. The claim is registered with direction -1 so that a positive signed result would indicate stronger STAT3 dependency in KEAP1-loss lines. A positive result would be a new potential primary association; a null result would reinforce that KEAP1 loss is strongly specific for the NFE2L2 dependency phenotype rather than a broad transcriptional-survival dependency effect.\n\nFinally, I test CDKN2A loss against USP7 dependency. TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency, and CDKN2A alteration can affect p53-axis stress signaling through ARF loss. The claim direction is +1: a positive signed estimate would indicate weaker USP7 dependency in CDKN2A-loss lines, analogous to the TP53-loss USP7 direction. The expected raw direction is uncertain. A null result would sharpen the specificity of TP53-loss USP7 dependency and separate the CDKN2A-MDM2 association from a broader USP7/p53-axis effect.\n\nAcross these proposals, I will treat signed dependency-score contrasts around 0.1 or larger, with intervals away from zero, as scientifically meaningful for this dependency-score scale, while recognizing that validated canonical effects here can be much larger. Any newly strong result should prompt either a context-specific follow-up or validation. All claims remain associations within this supplied dataset, not causal or generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Updated accepted/validated dataset-level set remains: TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency; KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency; SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency; PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 dependency; CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 dependency. H100 remains unresolved due inconclusive TP53-loss context validation. Nulls continue to support specificity: PIK3CA, STK11 main, APC pending, MYC effects mostly null except pending MYC-MDM2; RB1, NF1 for prior targets, EGFR/ERBB2 amplification main target effects null; MTAP and RPL11 null.\n\nTo allocate the next 12 analyses, I defer H21, H22, H23, H45, H46, and H59 (robustness/co-alteration refinements of accepted associations) because many robustness contexts are already supported. Target next panel: existing active specificity tests H64, H65, H68, H125, H127, H128 plus six new probes: MYC amplification -> MDM2 within TP53-wt; PTEN loss -> MDM2 within TP53-wt; NF1 loss -> STAT3; ERBB2 amplification -> STAT3; KEAP1 loss -> STAT3; CDKN2A loss -> USP7. This balances discovery (possible new MDM2 or STAT3 axis effects) with specificity (whether accepted associations extend beyond their current outcomes). If a new effect reaches the practical dependency benchmark, consider validation; otherwise continue mapping specificity. All conclusions remain dataset-level associations."
