  "narrative": "H150 validation synthesis. H150 tests CDKN2A loss versus CDKN2A-intact lines for MDM2 dependency within TP53-loss and MTAP-wildtype lines. The claim direction is -1, so a positive signed estimate corresponds to a raw exposed-minus-comparator difference that is more negative, i.e. stronger MDM2 dependency. Discovery R130 was +0.0998 with a 95% Welch interval +0.018 to +0.181. The independent validation R142 is now available: signed estimate +0.1459 with independent adjusted interval +0.0231 to +0.2687. At the run-wide adjusted alpha of 0.00385, this interval excludes zero, and the point estimate is larger than the discovery estimate and comfortably above the practical effect scale I have been using for these continuous dependency scores. Although the lower bound is imprecise and does not tightly constrain the magnitude above a threshold, the direction, statistical evidence, and effect size support the refined dataset association: within TP53-loss lines that are MTAP-wildtype, CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency. I therefore accept H150 and close this investigation. This does not retroactively establish the broader TP53-loss-only context H100, whose independent validation crossed zero and remains closed unresolved. It does show that the MDM2-CDKN2A association can persist in a cleaner TP53-loss/MTAP-wildtype context and clarifies that H100 should not be interpreted as unsupported broadly in TP53-wildtype lines, which remain strongly supported by H99/H110.\n\nCurrent supported dataset associations, primarily in the supplied lung-cell-line screen dataset: TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency; KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency; SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high is associated with stronger WRN dependency; PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency; and CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency overall. All of these large primary associations have independent validation support: USP7 H7/R7 and R92; NFE2L2 H13/R9 and R26; SMARCA2 H10/R10 and R39; WRN H8/R8 and R52; STAT3 H67/R55 and R65; MDM2 H94/R62 and R78. They are also robust across several tested contexts, including lineage subtype, culture type, omics availability, serum, ploidy/aneuploidy, Cas9 activity, screen quality, and selected co-alteration backgrounds. The newly accepted H150 refines the MDM2 association by confirming it in TP53-loss/MTAP-wildtype lines.\n\nNegative and specificity findings are substantial. Most canonical driver/RTK genotype tests were null or very small for the corresponding dependencies: KRAS mutation did not show meaningful KRAS dependency difference, BRAF V600E did not show meaningful BRAF dependency difference, EGFR amplification did not show meaningful EGFR dependency, ERBB2 amplification did not show meaningful ERBB2 dependency, and NF1 loss was not meaningfully associated with RIT1 dependency in the tested contrasts. Cross-pathway probes generally did not extend the accepted associations to unrelated dependencies: KEAP1 loss did not extend to SMARCA2, SMARCA4 loss did not extend to USP7 or POLR2A, TP53 loss did not extend to WRN or NFE2L2, PTEN loss did not extend to USP7 or MDM2 in TP53-wildtype lines, and many interaction tests with lineage subtype, screen batch, library, TP53 status, MTAP status, and screen quality were negative. Research markers D, E, and F were mostly null where tested. These negative findings increase confidence that the accepted associations are relatively specific dataset associations rather than broad technical or pathway artifacts.\n\nRemaining uncertainty is mainly contextual and mechanistic. The exact H100 TP53-loss-only CDKN2A-MDM2 context remains unresolved/closed because discovery was positive but validation was imprecise and crossed zero. MTAP co-loss within CDKN2A-loss lines, H160, is also unresolved/closed: no evidence supports an additional MTAP effect, but the interval is too wide to claim precise absence. Some high-ploidy, high-mutation-burden, and other conditional acceptances are robust but still depend on subgroup sample sizes and should not be generalized beyond the supplied dataset. Biological interpretation remains association-only: these analyses do not establish causality, therapeutic vulnerability, or external cell-line generalizability. The most informative next registered analyses are direct tests of whether the refined MDM2 association is modified by TP53 status among MTAP-wildtype lines, and whether the SMARCA4 loss association with stronger SMARCA2 dependency persists in ARID1A-wildtype lines. These address the most important remaining context-specific questions after the current validation.",
  "research_notes": "Updated after H150 validation. Accepted primary dataset associations with independent validation: TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency H7; KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency H13; SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency H10; MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency H8; PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 dependency H67; CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 dependency overall H94. New accepted refinement: CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 dependency within TP53-loss/MTAP-wildtype lines H150, supported by R130 and independent R142. H100 broader TP53-loss-only MDM2 context remains unresolved/closed because validation crossed zero. H160 MTAP co-loss within CDKN2A-loss remains unresolved/closed; no evidence of added effect. Additional conditional acceptances include SMARCA2 in adherent culture, matched omics, and ARID1A-loss; WRN in adenocarcinoma, high serum, near-diploid, and high screen QC; USP7 in adenocarcinoma, Avana/non-Avana, high QC, and high Cas9; STAT3 in TP53-wildtype, TP53-loss, high screen QC, and high ploidy; MDM2 in TP53-wildtype, MTAP-wildtype, high screen QC, and high mutation burden. All conclusions remain association-only in the supplied dataset. Remaining validations reserved. Next registered analyses: MDM2 CDKN2A by TP53 interaction among MTAP-wildtype lines, and SMARCA4 loss effect in ARID1A-wildtype lines."
}

