ronger MDM2 dependency overall, H94 R62 +0.117 and R78 +0.130. The newly accepted H150 refines the MDM2 association by showing persistence in a TP53-loss, MTAP-wildtype background.\n\nSeveral context-specific acceptances further show that the accepted associations are not confined to one technical or biological stratum in this dataset. TP53-loss USP7 effects persist in adenocarcinoma H16/R80, Avana library H44/R83, non-Avana library H50/R81, high screen quality H73/R82, and high Cas9 activity H156/R136. KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 effects persist within TP53-loss H56/R35, within KEAP1-loss USP7 context H57/R36, and in high aneuploidy H158/R138. SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 effects persist in TP53-wildtype, KEAP1-wildtype, MSI-wildtype lines H31/R17, TP53-loss lines H58/R37, high screen quality H74/R90, adherent culture H151/R131, matched RNA-DNA omics H152/R132, and ARID1A-loss H153/R133. MSI-high WRN effects persist in adenocarcinoma H15/R141, TP53- and KEAP1-wildtype lines H32/R18, high screen quality H75/R91, high serum H154/R134, and near-diploid backgrounds H155/R135. PTEN-loss STAT3 effects persist in TP53-wildtype H96/R84, TP53-loss H97/R66, high screen quality H104/R94, and high ploidy H157/R137. CDKN2A-loss MDM2 effects persist in TP53-wildtype lines H99/R68, high screen quality H105/R73, MTAP-wildtype lines H110/R77, and high mutation burden H159/R139, and now in the TP53-loss, MTAP-wildtype context H150/R130 and R142.\n\nThe negative and specificity findings are also substantial. Most canonical driver or receptor-tyrosine-kinase genotype probes were near null for the corresponding dependencies: KRAS mutation did not show a meaningful KRAS-dependency difference H1/R1, BRAF V600E did not show a meaningful BRAF-dependency difference H2/R2, EGFR amplification did not show a validated practical EGFR-dependency difference H3/R3 and R13, ERBB2 amplification did not show a meaningful ERBB2-dependency difference H4/R4, and NF1 loss was not meaningfully associated with RIT1 dependency H5/R5 or other tested dependencies H109/R76, H118/R97, H136/R119. Cross-pathway probes generally did not extend accepted associations to unrelated dependencies: KEAP1 loss did not meaningfully affect USP7 H41/R24 or SMARCA2 H64/R106; SMARCA4 loss did not meaningfully affect USP7 H65/R107, WRN H42/R25, or POLR2A H141/R124; TP53 loss did not meaningfully affect WRN H144/R125, NFE2L2 H146/R126, STAT3 H95/R93, RPL11 H48/R53, or DHX9 in the practical hypothesized direction H91/R63; PTEN loss did not meaningfully affect USP7 H103/R72 or MDM2 in TP53-wildtype lines H130/R113. MYC amplification was null for DHX9 H11/R11, POLR2A H12/R12, RPL11 H68/R108, KIF18A H88/R59, and STAT3 H87/R56. BRCA2 loss was null for WRN H107/R74 and DHX9 H108/R75; MSI-high was null for DHX9 H113/R87 and POLR2A H114/R88; RB1 loss was null for KIF18A H69/R57 and WRN H117/R96; CDKN2A loss was null for KIF18A H70/R58 and RPL11 H116/R95. Many interaction tests were negative, including lineage subtype H25/R27, H26/R28, H27/R29, H28/R30, screen batch H52/R31, H53/R32, H54/R33, H55/R34, screen-quality interactions H80/R44, H81/R45, H82/R46, H83/R47, co-alteration interactions H76/R40, H77/R41, H78/R42, H79/R43, H84/R48, H85/R49, H86/R50, H98/R67, H101/R70, H120/R99, H121/R100, H122/R101, H123/R102, H124/R103, and library/library-context probes H33/R19, H34/R20, H35/R21 and R79, H20/R14. Research markers D, E, and F were mostly null where tested, including H18/R22, H20/R14, and H36/R23. These nulls increase confidence that the accepted large effects are relatively specific dataset associations rather than broad technical, lineage, or assay artifacts.\n\nRemaining uncertainty is mainly contextual. H100, the exact TP53-loss-only CDKN2A-MDM2 comparison, remains unresolved/closed: discovery R69 was positive but independent validation R105 crossed zero. H160 remains unresolved/closed: within CDKN2A-loss lines, MTAP co-loss did not show a credible additional effect, but the interval R140 +0.064, -0.025 to +0.153, is too wide to claim precise absence. Several conditional acceptances are robust but depend on subgroup sample sizes and should not be generalized beyond the supplied lung-cell-line dataset. The most informative next registered analyses are: first, an MDM2 CDKN2A-by-TP53 interaction among MTAP-wildtype lines, to test whether TP53 status still modifies the CDKN2A-MDM2 association after removing MTAP co-deletion confounding; and second, SMARCA4-loss association with stronger SMARCA2 dependency in ARID1A-wildtype lines, to test whether the SWI/SNF context is independent of ARID1A co-loss.",
