  "narrative": "All 12 newly delivered comparisons are valid Welch tests with at least 20 observations in each cell. The returned estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis directions, so positive signed values support the claim and negative signed values oppose it.\n\nH178, H179, and H180 test whether the strongest accepted main associations persist in PTEN-defined strata. H178 tests TP53 loss versus USP7 dependency in PTEN-intact lines: signed effect +0.801, 95% CI +0.780 to +0.822, cells 352/1382. H179 tests KEAP1 loss versus NFE2L2 dependency in PTEN-loss lines: signed effect +0.776, 95% CI +0.718 to +0.834, cells 63/203. H180 tests SMARCA4 loss versus SMARCA2 dependency in PTEN-loss lines: signed effect +0.754, 95% CI +0.707 to +0.801, cells 58/208. These are extremely large effects relative to the outcome distributions, intervals are far from zero, and the directions match the accepted parent associations. I accept all three as dataset-specific context-robust associations and close them.\n\nH181 and H182 test MSI-high versus WRN dependency in TP53-loss lines and higher-ploidy lines. H181: signed effect +0.833, 95% CI +0.765 to +0.900, cells 24/382. H182: signed effect +0.772, 95% CI +0.722 to +0.822, cells 57/704. The estimates are large and positive; despite the small exposed cell in H181, the interval is still far from zero and the parent MSI-WRN association has multiple independent and context robustness supports elsewhere. I accept both and close them.\n\nH183 and H184 test CDKN2A loss versus MDM2 dependency in high-aneuploidy and PTEN-loss contexts. H183: signed effect +0.120, 95% CI +0.044 to +0.197, cells 93/340. H184: signed effect +0.125, 95% CI +0.029 to +0.220, cells 51/215. These are not as enormous as the core gene-loss associations, but they exceed the practical 0.1 dependency-unit benchmark, exclude zero, and are large relative to the MDM2 dependency distribution. Given that CDKN2A-MDM2 was already accepted and independently validated overall, I accept these two refined contexts and close them, while noting that the lower bounds are modest enough to justify a dataset-specific rather than generalizable claim.\n\nH185 tests whether MTAP co-loss adds signal within CDKN2A-loss, TP53-wildtype lines. The signed estimate is only +0.065, 95% CI -0.034 to +0.163, cells 68/252. The interval includes zero and the point estimate is small. There is no evidence that MTAP co-loss produces a meaningful additional MDM2 dependency effect in this specific co-alteration context. I reject and close H185; this does not overturn the broader CDKN2A-MDM2 findings, but it indicates no detectable MTAP co-loss modification here.\n\nH45, H46, H49, and H72 test library-stratum robustness. H45 SMARCA4 loss versus SMARCA2 dependency in Avana: signed effect +0.781, 95% CI +0.760 to +0.802, cells 351/1414. H46 MSI-high versus WRN dependency in Avana: signed effect +0.794, 95% CI +0.764 to +0.825, cells 120/1645. H49 KEAP1 loss versus NFE2L2 dependency in Avana: signed effect +0.796, 95% CI +0.774 to +0.818, cells 353/1412. H72 KEAP1 loss versus NFE2L2 dependency in non-Avana: signed effect +0.794, 95% CI +0.746 to +0.842, cells 40/195. All are large, positive, and exclude zero. These results argue strongly against the core SMARCA2, WRN, and NFE2L2 associations being artifacts of Avana versus non-Avana library composition in this dataset. I accept and close them.\n\nOverall, this batch strengthens the core interpretation that the data support a limited set of highly consistent gene-loss or genomic-state associations with specific dependency scores, rather than broad canonical mutation or receptor-amplification effects. The evidence remains associations within the supplied lung cell-line dataset; conditioning on PTEN, TP53, ploidy, aneuploidy, and library status does not establish causality or external generalizability. Multiplicity remains a concern, but the accepted effects are large, consistent, and repeatedly supported across independent strata, making accidental subgroup artifacts less likely. I do not request validation because no new voluntary validation requests remain.",
  "research_notes": "Updated after iteration 14 appraise. Core accepted, independently or strongly context-supported dataset-specific associations: TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency; KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency; SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency; PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency; CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2 dependency. New accepted context robustness from R169-R175 and R177-R180: USP7/PT53 persists in PTEN-intact lines; NFE2L2/KEAP1 persists in PTEN-loss and both Avana/non-Avana strata; SMARCA2/SMARCA4 persists in PTEN-loss and Avana strata; WRN/MSI-high persists in TP53-loss, high-ploidy, and Avana strata; MDM2/CDKN2A persists in high-aneuploidy and PTEN-loss contexts. H185 is rejected: no evidence that MTAP co-loss adds to CDKN2A-MDM2 within CDKN2A-loss TP53-wildtype lines; interval includes zero and effect is modest. Negative/null specificity results from prior iterations and this batch indicate canonical driver mutations, receptor amplifications, many cross-pathway probes, and arbitrary marker assays are largely not associated with the studied dependencies, except the robust gene-loss/driver-state effects listed above. No validation requests remain. To finish useful registered specificity and robustness testing without validation, activate H51 non-Avana direct SMARCA4-SMARCA2, H60 sex interaction TP53-USP7, H61 adherent-culture interaction SMARCA4-SMARCA2, H62 high-serum interaction MSI-WRN, H89/H90 TMED10 EGFR/PIK3CA probes, H142 DHX9-KEAP1, H143 KIF18A-STK11, and H145 WRN-ARID1A. These remaining claims are exploratory specificity/broad-panel tests; strong null results are informative but would not overturn the accepted core associations. All conclusions remain within the supplied dataset and do not imply causality or external generalizability."
}

