  "narrative": "The newly delivered discovery results are the registered TP53-loss and PTEN-loss specificity panel. All estimates are already signed to direction -1, so a positive signed value supports the hypothesis that the loss state is associated with stronger dependency, while values near zero or negative do not. The TP53-loss comparisons for BRAF, EGFR, ERBB2, KRAS, RIT1, and TMED10 (H222-H227/R217-R222) produce extremely small signed estimates, ranging from about -0.017 to +0.006, and every 95% Welch interval includes zero. None reaches a practically meaningful fraction of the dependency-score scale, and none survives the family-wise uncertainty from this exploratory panel. I therefore reject these proposed associations: these data do not support TP53 loss as a broad modifier of dependency across these outcomes beyond the previously accepted USP7 association. The PTEN-loss comparisons for RPL11, TMED10, KRAS, BRAF, ERBB2, and DHX9 (H228-H233/R223-R228) are likewise generally null, with signed estimates from about -0.013 to +0.023 and all intervals crossing zero. H231/R226 is the only borderline case: PTEN loss versus BRAF dependency has signed estimate +0.023, 95% CI -0.0001 to 0.046. It is nominally close to zero, but the interval includes null, the nominal p-value would not survive adjustment for this 12-test specificity panel, and the point estimate is only about 9% relative to the overall absolute BRAF dependency score, below the 10% practical relevance threshold. I therefore do not accept it and record it as rejected/closed rather than allowing a weak borderline signal to expand the accepted PTEN specificity map. Overall, these results strengthen marker specificity: TP53 loss remains associated with USP7 dependency, not a general pattern across these dependency outcomes; PTEN loss remains associated with STAT3 dependency, not with RPL11, TMED10, KRAS, BRAF, ERBB2, or DHX9 in this dataset. No leakage is apparent from these simple binary-marker tests, but 