  "narrative": "All twelve due estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction, so for the MDM2 CDKN2A-loss tests with direction -1 a positive estimate means CDKN2A-loss lines are associated with stronger, more negative MDM2 dependency, and for the STAT3 PTEN-loss tests a positive estimate means PTEN-loss lines are associated with stronger STAT3 dependency. H234/R229 shows a robust MDM2 CDKN2A contrast in TP53-loss, high-screen-quality screens: signed estimate +0.132 with 95% Welch interval [0.045, 0.218], cell counts 67 and 244. This is a medium-to-large effect on the MDM2 score scale and comfortably exceeds the 10-percent practical yardstick, so I accept H234 and close it. This supports the prior accepted core effect rather than a qualitatively new finding, but it substantially reduces uncertainty about the TP53-loss context by showing that attenuation in unfiltered TP53-loss screens is not evidence against the association in high-quality screens. H253/R230 similarly supports MDM2 CDKN2A contrast in TP53-loss, MTAP-negative, high-quality screens: +0.122 [0.030, 0.215], n=57 and 199. The interval excludes zero and the lower bound remains near the practical threshold, so I accept H253 and close it. This refines the previously validated TP53-loss/MTAP-negative MDM2 context and again argues that earlier uncertainty was driven partly by precision and screening context, not biological absence. H235/R231 tests CDKN2A-loss MDM2 dependency in MYC-amplified high-quality screens: +0.151 [0.044, 0.258], n=53 and 176. Despite a small exposed cell, the interval excludes zero and the estimate is practically meaningful. I accept H235 and close it. This does not make MYC amplification a main effect on MDM2, but it supports that the CDKN2A-loss MDM2 association persists in a stringent MYC-amplified quality subset and resolves the concern that the prior MYC-amplification unresolved result was solely a screening artifact. H254/R232 tests CDKN2A-loss MDM2 dependency in high-MYC-expression, high-quality screens: +0.133 [0.052, 0.214], n=81 and 302. I accept H254 and close it as a robust refinement of the already accepted high-MYC-expression MDM2 context. Taken together, H234, H253, H235, and H254 strengthen the core MDM2/CDKN2A association by showing that it remains detectable under several biologically demanding and technically stringent conditions. H241/R233 tests whether screen quality modifies the MDM2 CDKN2A effect. The signed interaction is +0.061 with interval [-0.015, 0.138], cell counts 304, 1195, 97, and 404. The interval includes zero and does not establish a clinically meaningful interaction, although the upper bound is not negligible and the test cannot prove exact equality across quality strata. I reject H241 as an established interaction and close it; the appropriate conclusion is absence of evidence for meaningful effect modification by this screen-quality split, not proof that no modification exists. H242/R234 tests MDM2 CDKN2A dependency in Avana screens: +0.116 [0.079, 0.153], n=354 and 1411. I accept and close it. H243/R235 tests non-Avana screens: +0.125 [0.029, 0.222], n=47 and 188. The interval excludes zero and the lower bound is near the practical threshold, so I accept H243 and close it. Because the non-Avana exposed cell is small, the estimate is less precise than in Avana screens, but the result supports library robustness. H244/R236 tests adenocarcinoma-only MDM2 CDKN2A dependency: +0.114 [0.074, 0.155], n=290 and 1132. I accept and close it, showing that the association is not driven by the minority non-adenocarcinoma lineage-subtype group. H245/R237 tests STAT3 PTEN-loss dependency in Avana screens: +0.288 [0.232, 0.343], n=239 and 1526. This is large on the STAT3 scale, excludes zero with substantial margin, and supports robustness of the accepted STAT3/PTEN association in the dominant library. I accept and close H245. H246/R238 tests STAT3 PTEN-loss dependency in non-Avana screens: +0.092 [-0.062, 0.246], n=27 and 208. The estimate is suggestive but the exposed cell is only just above the minimum and the interval is wide, encompassing no effect, attenuation, and a clinically meaningful positive effect. I do not accept the non-Avana-specific claim, nor do I reject the association; I leave it unresolved and close this particular library-restricted question because the supplied dataset does not provide enough non-Avana PTEN-loss information to resolve it. The overall STAT3/PTEN claim remains accepted based on H67, H245, and other robustness evidence, but its library-specific non-Avana estimate remains uncertain. H240/R239 tests whether PIK3CA co-mutation modifies STAT3 dependency in PTEN-loss lines. The interaction is essentially null at +0.001 [-0.150, 0.152], with a very small exposed co-mutation cell (n=38). The interval includes zero and the point estimate is negligible; there is no evidence supporting this interaction. I reject H240 and close it as a failed biological modifier test, while noting the test is underpowered and cannot exclude all possible PIK3CA effect modification. H248/R240 tests whether high MYC expression modifies the NFE2L2/KEAP1-loss association. The signed interaction is small at +0.033 [-0.014, 0.081], with cell counts 92, 419, 301, and 1188. The interval includes zero and the magnitude is small relative to the large main NFE2L2/KEAP1-loss effect and the dependency-score scale. I reject H248 as an established interaction and close it. From a leakage and confounding standpoint, these subgroup and interaction tests use measured predictors and QC variables rather than outcome-derived selection, so direct leakage is not apparent. However, all marker states are observed nonrandomly and may co-occur with broader genomic instability, mutation burden, lineage features, or screening context. The accepted MDM2 and STAT3 context results are therefore associations in this dataset and do not establish causality or generalizable mechanism. Multiplicity is substantial, especially in the MDM2 refinement family. I accept individual context claims because they are pre-registered refinements of a previously validated core effect, intervals exclude zero, and effects exceed the practical threshold; I do not accept the interaction tests because their intervals include zero or their magnitudes are not meaningful. No validation request is possible because the voluntary validation budget is exhausted."
